memorial","","","","n5363381002"],["Mount Gilead Veterans Memorial","memorial","","","","n4727649695"],["Harding Birthplace","memorial","Warren G. Harding, 29th President of the United States was born on this site on November 2, 1865.","","","n5313763671"],["Former Leland Hotel site","memorial","This is the former site of the 9-story Leland Hotel that was constructed in 1927 by architect Vernon Redding. The hotel was the tallest building in Mansfield, Ohio when it was completed. The Leland Hotel was razed in 1976 to make way for a parking lot.","1927","","w1016819216"],["Johnny Appleseed Monument","monument","","","","n10971952104"],["Verterans Memorial","memorial","","","","n10974219390"],["Mohican-Memorial Shrine","memorial","The Mohican-Memorial Shrine is the state's official monument to her nearly 20,000 sons and daughters who died in those conflicts.","","https://ohiodnr.gov/go-and-do/plan-a-visit/find-a-property/mohicanmemorial-shrine","w466262044"],["Mohican Memorial Forest Shrine","memorial","","","","w1341814259"],["General Grant Monument","monument","","","","n10605121206"],["Historical plaque","plaque","","","","n8045514094"],["Robert J Kurtzman Jr. Memorial Plaque","plaque","","","","n7042298121"],["John H. Glenn, Jr.","memorial","","","","n13087729345"],["Union School","plaque","","","","n14004675404"],["Veterans' Memorial","memorial","","","","n10303287918"],["Fort Laurens State Memorial","memorial","","","","r19508780"],["Ohio & Erie Canal - Lock 7","ruins","","","","w1307317176"],["Ohio & Erie Canal - Lock 8","ruins","","","","w1307317175"],["Ohio & Erie Canal - Lock 9","ruins","","","","w1307317174"],["Thurman Lee Munson memorial","memorial","","","","n11849828760"],["Ohio & Erie Canal - Lock 10","ruins","","","","w1307317173"],["Zoar Cemeter","plaque","Zoar Cemetery, known as “God’s Acre,” is the final resting place of Zoar Separatists and their descendants. Early graves had no headstones, reflecting the belief that all were equal in death. The cemetery includes victims of the 1834 cholera epidemic.","2006","https://remarkableohio.org/marker/18-79-zoar-cemetery/","n14111126171"],["Ohio and Erie Canal","plaque","Zoar Separatists built this section of the Ohio & Erie Canal in 1825–27, earning $21,000. The canal connected Zoar to markets until railroads reduced its use. Today, its towpath serves as a recreational trail alongside historic locks and mills.","2002","https://remarkableohio.org/marker/13-79-ohio-and-erie-canal/","n14111126169"],["Cow Barn Foundation","ruins","","","","w1551209480"],["Zoar Town Hall","plaque","Built in 1887, Zoar Town Hall reflects the village’s history as a successful communal society founded by German religious dissidents in 1817. Zoarites also built seven miles of the Ohio & Erie Canal, helping establish the community’s economic success.","2000","https://remarkableohio.org/marker/9-79-zoar-town-hall-zoar-and-the-ohio-erie-canal/","n14111163030"],["Zoar Garden","plaque","The Zoar Garden, established by the Separatists by 1829, was both a beautiful pleasure garden and a symbol of their faith. Its layout represented the New Jerusalem, with Christ as the Tree of Life and paths symbolizing life’s journey toward Heaven.","2008","https://remarkableohio.org/marker/19-79-zoar-garden/","n14111126170"],["The Zoar Hotel","plaque","Built in 1833 by Zoar Separatists, the Zoar Hotel welcomed travelers, artists, and notable guests like William McKinley. It later became a restaurant before closing in 1983. Its exterior was restored in 2001–02.","2021","https://remarkableohio.org/marker/30-79-the-zoar-hotel/","n14107886298"],["Zoar Meeting House","plaque","Built in 1853 under leader Joseph M. Bimeler, the Zoar Meeting House served as the Separatists’ second house of worship. Men and women entered through separate doors. The building remains in continuous use and now houses the Zoar United Church of Christ.","2011","https://remarkableohio.org/marker/22-79-zoar-meeting-house/","n14111126172"],["North 